From e26da203a4d12109118a18a4f49cb7eae790b49f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Golle <daniel@makrotopia.org>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 23:54:17 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] media: rockchip: rkisp2: Read raw frames from VICAP on the
 RV1126

The raw readback input of the ISP is a video output node that user
space has to fill. On the RV1126 the VICAP driver offers its MIPI
stream to a consumer through a V4L2 subdevice, so the frames can be
passed on without user space.

Move the readback start and stop sequence and the buffer queueing into
helpers, bind the VICAP subdevice with an async notifier and feed every
completed frame to the first readback channel when the first capture
stream starts. Let the link from the first readback node to the ISP
decide who supplies the frames: the link is created disabled on SoCs
with a VICAP frame source and takes the VICAP input as long as it stays
disabled. Select the readback input before the first trigger, as the
hardware drops the trigger of the write that changes the input mode in
about 7 % of the stream starts.

The readback engine takes the physical address the VICAP DMA writes the
frame to. While the ISP IOMMU is on the identity domain that base
translates 1:1, but a translated domain attached to the ISP faults on
it, so every shared buffer is mapped into the current domain at its own
physical address and unmapped at release while the group is still on
that domain. Detaching or re-attaching the domain instead would
reprogram the SMMU while the ISP is streaming.

The readback input selection is normally made when the first capture
output starts streaming, and the in-kernel feed can run without any
capture output, so the VICAP start selects the readback input as well
and latches it with the soft update the capture path uses. A mi_raw
debugfs dump shows the readback configuration.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Daniel Golle <daniel@makrotopia.org>
